<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # There Are So Many Types of Clubs! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Start a Club!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> There Are So Many Types of Clubs!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate discussions on project goals and tasks. Text: Clarify written instructions in collaborative projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate group discussions by encouraging participation, clarifying ideas, and summarizing goals (e.g., "Let's brainstorm ideas for improving the club activities." "So far, we've agreed to organize a school clean-up day and a community reading event.").<br>Can clarify written instructions for organizing a club activity by providing a visual checklist (e.g., drawing steps on the board: "Step 1: Find members; Step 2: Choose activities").<br>Can help group members understand club tasks by rephrasing instructions (e.g., "Let's set up a meeting every week," while pointing to a calendar).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 47%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by asking students to recall and share one interesting club idea from previous lessons or a club they have joined. "Who remembers the club ideas we discussed? What kind of club would you like to join? Last year, I joined a sports club, and I made new friends." (Simple past, asking about preferences). This activates prior knowledge and sets a context for group discussion. The teacher introduces the idea of *explaining* and *clarifying* ideas to others, highlighting its importance in team projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to clarify an instruction. "If I say, 'Please get ready for the club meeting,' and someone looks confused, I can rephrase: 'Let's prepare our notes for the meeting.' Or, I can summarize a group's agreement: 'So far, we've agreed to organize a school clean-up day and a community reading event. Is that correct?' This demonstrates facilitating discussion and summarizing goals."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of mediation concepts.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'facilitate a discussion'?" (Expected: To help a group talk and make decisions, encouraging everyone to participate.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to 'clarify written instructions' in a group project like creating a club?" (Expected: To ensure everyone understands the tasks, avoid mistakes, and work collaboratively.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, slightly ambiguous written draft of an "Eco-Club Activity Plan" for a school clean-up event. The plan includes steps and roles, using some target vocabulary (e.g., *goals, activities, members, responsibilities, organize, coordinate*).<br>Genre: Activity Plan Draft. Register: Semi-formal. Format: Bulleted list with brief descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students identify unclear instructions or areas requiring discussion in a collaborative project plan, preparing them for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main *project* or *event* this Eco-Club is trying to *organize*?" (Expected: A school clean-up day).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify the Unclear:</b> Students read the plan and individually highlight or circle any instructions that seem vague or could lead to confusion. For example, "Get supplies" or "Clean the area."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Discussion Prompt:</b> "In pairs, discuss which parts of this plan need more *clarification* or *discussion* among the *team members*." (This transitions to the need for mediation.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice rephrasing written instructions, summarizing key points, and using imperatives for clear task delegation. Focus on grammar: simple present ("This club *is* for..."), imperatives ("*Set* goals," "*Let's plan* activities"), and target vocabulary (e.g., *manage, negotiate, plan, organize, create, engage, discuss, respond, coordinate, assign, delegate, evaluate, contribute, participate, schedule, arrange, support, facilitate, responsibilities, roles, meetings*). Pronunciation practice on contrastive sounds (e.g., /pr/ in "project" and /tr/ in "treasure"), stress on -ate verbs (e.g., "coordi*nate*," "negot*iate*"), and emphasizing imperatives (e.g., "<b>Set</b> goals!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Rephrasing for Clarity.</b> The teacher displays vague instructions from the Eco-Club plan (e.g., "Get supplies," "Clean the area"). Students, in pairs, rephrase each instruction, making it more specific. Teacher reminds them to use imperative verbs and descriptive nouns. Example: "Get supplies" becomes "<b>Collect</b> trash bags, gloves, and brooms from the janitor's office." or "<b>Let's arrange</b> for the necessary *equipment*." The teacher highlights stress on imperatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: Facilitating Discussion Phrases.</b> Students work in small groups. Each group receives a scenario where a team member is confused about a task (e.g., "I don't understand what 'assign *roles*' means"). One student acts as a 'Facilitator' and practices using mediation phrases to clarify, encourage discussion, and summarize. Phrases: "Can I *clarify* that for you?", "What *suggestions* do you have?", "So far, we've *agreed* to...", "Let's *brainstorm* ideas..." Teacher circulates, monitoring the use of target vocabulary (e.g., *delegate, responsibilities, coordinate*) and pronunciation of -ate verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check: Summarizing Goals.</b> Groups quickly review a short text describing the *goals* of another fictional club (e.g., "The Sports Club aims to foster teamwork and promote physical health."). One student reads, another *summarizes* the goals in their own words, and a third asks a *clarifying question* about the summary (e.g., "So, you're saying the main *goal* is to *participate* in team sports, not individual ones?"). Students practice sociolinguistic skills by responding to feedback and suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group discussion and collaborative refinement of their "Club Creation Plan" (21st Century Skills Project). Students will act as facilitators within their groups to ensure understanding, encourage participation, and clarify written instructions for their plan, integrating the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students apply mediation skills to finalize the step-by-step guide for starting a school club. They will facilitate discussions, clarify each step, and ensure all *members* understand their *responsibilities* and the overall *goals* of their proposed club.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in facilitating discussions, clarifying written instructions, summarizing group decisions, encouraging *participation*, and using appropriate language for coordination (simple present, imperatives, relevant vocabulary). Pronunciation focuses on stress in imperatives like "<b>Set</b> goals!" and contrastive sounds in words like *project* and *participate*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Club Plan Facilitation & Feedback Session."</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students return to their "Club Creation Plan" groups. Their main task is to review and finalize their step-by-step guide, ensuring it is clear and actionable for any future *members*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group designates a "Facilitator" for a specific section of their plan (e.g., one facilitates "Defining Club Goals," another "Planning Activities," etc.). This role rotates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The Facilitator's job is to:
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Present a section of the written "Club Creation Plan" (e.g., "Step 1: Define Goals and Interests").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ask open-ended questions to *engage* the group and *facilitate* discussion (e.g., "What *suggestions* do you have for improving our club's *goals*?").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Clarify* any *written instructions* or concepts that seem unclear (e.g., "When we say 'create a *structure*,' we mean to *assign* *roles* and *responsibilities*.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Summarize* group agreements and next steps (e.g., "So, we've *agreed* to add a 'community outreach' *goal* to our *plan*.").</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other group members practice asking about club preferences ("What type of club would you like to join?"), narrating stories ("Last year, I joined a sports club, and I made new friends."), and negotiating roles/responsibilities ("I would like to be the treasurer because I am good at math."), using simple present to describe preferences ("I like this club because we can use any room to work together.") and simple past for experiences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing effective mediation, use of target language, and pronunciation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for mediation skills and peer assessment during group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents one key section of their finalized "Club Creation Plan" (e.g., "Our Club's Goals" or "Our First Activity Plan"). During this presentation, they must explicitly demonstrate mediation skills by:
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarifying one specific written instruction or goal for the class.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitating a very brief discussion (e.g., inviting one peer question and responding to it).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizing their group's final decision for that section.</li>
                </ol></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation (Text):</b> Student effectively clarifies a written instruction, rephrasing or providing examples (e.g., drawing steps on the board: "Step 1: Find members; Step 2: Choose activities").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effectiveness of Facilitation (Communication):</b> Student encourages participation, summarizes goals (e.g., "So far, we've agreed to organize a school clean-up day and a community reading event.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Appropriate and consistent use of simple present, imperatives, and key vocabulary (e.g., *facilitate, coordinate, assign, responsibilities, goals, plan*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation, with an effort towards correct stress on imperatives and "-ate" verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and Responsiveness:</b> Demonstrates ability to respond clearly to feedback and suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two reflective prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one instruction or idea you helped clarify for your group today when working on the "Club Creation Plan"? How did you do it?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did you contribute to summarizing your group's decisions or moving the discussion forward?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[7-8 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new strategies did I learn today for clarifying information or facilitating a group discussion?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can my mediation skills help me in other academic projects or real-life situations where clear communication is essential?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What mediation strategies (e.g., rephrasing, visual checklists, explicit summarization) worked best today for helping students clarify project goals and instructions for their "Club Creation Plan"?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed to further support students in confidently facilitating discussions and summarizing complex ideas in future collaborative projects?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Let's Start a Club!" theme, with students having finalized their "Club Creation Plan" through mediation. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, building upon the collaborative, communication, and mediation skills developed here, perhaps by challenging students to mediate cultural information or present their club plans to a larger, external audience (e.g., school board), requiring advanced mediation of their ideas and proposals.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>